// TWO request-correlated `since` policies in ONE policy set — the
// multi-policy path. An implementation that evaluates every temporal leaf of a
// policy set together must keep each leaf's correlated state separate, since
// nothing else distinguishes them.
//
// Both leaves correlate on `context.input.user`, so their guard streaks are
// keyed by the SAME correlation value — the per-policy identity is the ONLY
// thing separating dave's Transfer streak (policy 0's breaker) from dave's
// Refund streak (policy 1's breaker). The guard predicates differ (Transfer vs
// Refund), so any cross-policy read flips a verdict:
//
//   @20 Approve dave — dave DID transfer (@10) → deny.
//   @30 Review dave  — dave never refunded → PERMIT; if policy 1's lookup
//                      read policy 0's state, it would see dave's Transfer
//                      streak and wrongly deny.
//   @50 Review eve   — eve DID refund (@40) → deny.
//   @60 Approve eve  — eve never transferred → PERMIT; the mirror detector
//                      for leakage in the other direction.
//
// Every single-policy correlated case involves only one policy, so this is the
// only coverage of isolation between policies for the correlation machinery.
// Expected verdicts are in expected_1.out.
permit (
    principal,
    action == Drupe::Action::"Approve",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    !Drupe::Action::"Transfer"::request{ input.user: context.input.user }
    since within 1h
    Drupe::Action::"Login"::request{ input.user: "carol" }
};

permit (
    principal,
    action == Drupe::Action::"Review",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    !Drupe::Action::"Refund"::request{ input.user: context.input.user }
    since within 1h
    Drupe::Action::"Login"::request{ input.user: "carol" }
};
